{
  "summary": "Refactors validation and block storage functions across Bitcoin Core to return detailed error status and flush outcomes via util::Result and FlushResult rather than relying solely on AbortNode shutdowns. Touched subsystems include block writing, block index loading, coins state flushes, mempool transaction acceptance, assumeutxo snapshot activation, block connection, and chain activation. Upstream call sites throughout node initialization, RPC, and validation are updated and annotated with nodiscard attributes to enforce error checking.",
  "problem": "Validation and block storage routines currently handle fatal errors like disk write or flush failures by calling AbortNode directly to initiate asynchronous shutdown without passing error context back to callers. This makes robust error handling in libbitcoinkernel difficult, forcing embedded library callers to register global notification callbacks rather than handling errors programmatically at the call site.",
  "evidence": [
    "PR description: 'There are many validation functions that handle failures by calling AbortNode and triggering shutdowns, without returning error information to their callers. This makes error handling in libbitcoinkernel application code difficult, because the only way to handle these errors is to register for notification callbacks.'",
    "PR description: 'This PR is a pure refactoring that returns extra result information from functions without changing their behavior. It's a possible alternative to and subset of #29642, which adds similar return information but also makes behavior changes and exposes a FatalError type.'"
  ],
  "dependencies": {
    "depends_on": [
      "#25665"
    ],
    "enables": []
  },
  "scope_notes": "This is a large, invasive refactoring of core validation and blockstorage error handling across 56 files that relies on util::Result extensions introduced in base PR #25665.",
  "changed_since_previous": "Rebased onto the updated base PR, added CI workarounds for compiler maybe-uninitialized false positives on Windows and previous-release jobs, and temporarily increased Git fetch depth in CI workflows.",
  "card": "This pull request refactors validation and block storage functions that can hit fatal errors to bubble up detailed error status and flush state via util::Result and FlushResult instead of relying solely on AbortNode shutdowns. Currently, fatal failures like disk write errors trigger background shutdown callbacks without returning error context to the calling code, complicating error handling for applications consuming libbitcoinkernel. The author notes this is a pure refactor preserving node behavior designed to eliminate notification callback dependencies for library users. It depends on base PR #25665.",
